 lpsk31:
 =======
 lpsk31 is a ncurses-based Linux console application for Amateur Radio
 communications in the PSK31 digital mode. lpsk31 detects the carrier
 phase changes that make a PSK31-encoded character by reading audio
 samples from the DSP in the computer's sound card, connected to the
 output of a receiver. The audio signal is processed by a software phase
 detector which, for BPSK, detects '1' or '0' states ('0' signaled by a
 180 deg phase reversal) and the resulting stream of BPSK31-coded bits
 is decoded into ASCII characters for printing to the Receive window of
 lpsk31. In QPSK the carrier phase changes are processed by a
 soft-decision Viterbi decoder which provides error correction in bad
 receiving conditions.

 PLEASE NOTE that from version 0.8 the lpsk31rc file has changed slightly.
 Please read the version history in the HTML manual doc/lpsk31.html for
 more details.

 PLEASE READ the HTML manual doc/lpsk31.html for installation instructions.

 Configuration
 ------------

 lpsk31 reads its runtime configuration from a key = value file named
 lpsk31rc, located in the user's XDG config directory
 ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lpsk31/lpsk31rc, default ~/.config/lpsk31/lpsk31rc).
 If not found there, lpsk31 checks each directory in $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS
 (default /etc/xdg) and finally the legacy ~/lpsk31/lpsk31rc location,
 from which it auto-migrates the file on first run. See the
 "lpsk31rc configuration" section of the manual for details and the
 default/lpsk31rc file for a reference.

 Rig control
 ----------

 lpsk31's rig control is built on top of the Hamlib library. It works
 with any rig supported by Hamlib either by connecting directly to a
 device (set `model` and `device` in the `[rig]` section) or by
 talking to a running rigctld server (set only `device = host:port`).

 When enabled, lpsk31 sets the rig to USB or USB-D (digital USB; e.g.
 PKTUSB in rigctl terms) for BPSK and QPSK-USB, LSB or its digital
 equivalent for QPSK-LSB, in both transmit and receive direction; no
 CW mode is forced. Toggle rig control with the 't' key at runtime.

 Neoklis Kyriazis  15 August 2002


